  <h2>You don't have to run your business like everyone else does</h2>
  <p>Most of us start a business by borrowing someone else's blueprint. It makes sense, we've spent our whole lives being taught to follow instructions, get the gold star, and do things "the right way." So when we go out on our own, the instinct is to find someone who's already done it and copy their process.</p>
  <p>The problem, as Katherine puts it, is that those systems were never built with your energy or your needs in mind. What works for someone who thrives on being on camera every day, taking back-to-back calls, or posting constantly isn't automatically going to work for you, especially if you're more introverted, more sensitive to burnout, or just wired differently.</p>
  <p>That doesn't mean you get a free pass to do nothing. It means the goal isn't finding the one "right" strategy, it's finding the one that's right for you, and then actually doing it consistently.</p>
  <blockquote>"You don't have to do all of the things. You don't even have to do the popular things that everyone's doing or what's trending right now, but you still have to do something."</blockquote>

  <h2>Take stock before you plan the rest of the year</h2>
  <p>We're past the halfway mark of the year, which is exactly when the panic can set in about goals that felt exciting in January and haven't been looked at since. Before you scramble to catch up, take a step back first.</p>
  <p>Katherine's approach: take a real break, even just a week, from the day-to-day, and actually review what's happened so far. What worked? What felt exhausting for the results it produced? What did you do that you got no traction on at all? That review is what tells you what to keep doing, what to drop, and where you actually need to focus for the rest of the year.</p>
  <p>It's tempting to just keep adding new strategies on top of old ones, Katherine compares it to piling more and more onto your plate at a buffet because everything looks good. But you don't need to try everything at once. You can always go back for seconds later.</p>

  <h2>Plan in 90 days, not 12 months</h2>
  <p>A full year of planning can feel impossible to commit to, especially if your priorities or energy shift as often as Katherine's do. Instead, she works in 90-day chunks: one clear focus for the quarter, list growth, a launch, a specific offer, broken down into a milestone for each month, and then into weekly and daily steps.</p>
  <p>The daily part matters more than it sounds like it should. Instead of locking in a rigid to-do list weeks in advance, check in with your actual capacity each morning and adjust from there. Some weeks you'll have the energy for a big content push. Other weeks you won't, and that's fine, as long as your quarter is built to flex around that instead of falling apart because of it.</p>

  <h3>Know what actually moves the needle</h3>
  <p>None of this means abandoning goals or metrics. Katherine still sets a target and a stretch target for every launch or growth push, but the real value is in what you learn along the way. If you know it costs you 50 cents to get a lead from ads, or that a bundle collaboration brought in 200 new subscribers, you're no longer guessing next quarter. You know what input gets you the result you want, and you can repeat it, adjust it, or scale it on purpose.</p>
  <p>That's the difference between reacting to whatever's trending this week and actually building something that compounds.</p>

